---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ChapterNav from '../components/ChapterNav.astro';
/* R186 [§S9b]: the assumptions behind the unit count, rendered from the same
   data the allocation runs on. Every figure in the method note below used to
   be typed prose beside literals in units-client.ts, and a reader had no way
   to tell a measured input from a chosen one. src/lib/units.ts carries the
   grade and the owner; this page shows both. Static HTML, so it is what a
   crawler indexes and what a reader with no script sees. */
import {
  ALLOCATION_THRESHOLDS, allocateUnits, NATIONAL_OFFICE_VISITS, NETWORK_ABSORPTION,
  pct, UNIT_TYPE_KEYS, UNIT_TYPES, VISIT_SPLITS
} from '../lib/units';
import { countyDemand } from '../lib/counties';
import { gradedUnitRows } from '../lib/manifest-check';
/* R87 R211 R212 R88 R213 [§S9c]: every figure the regional model section
   states about its own result, computed here from the shipped model file.
   Each one used to be prose, and the two that were numbers were the two the
   audit could check. */
import { regionModel } from '../lib/region-data';
import {
  fragmentationAnchor, regionGeometry, selectionMargin, weightIntervals,
  FRAGILE_WITHIN
} from '../lib/hospital-regions';
const THROUGHPUTS = UNIT_TYPE_KEYS.map((k) => UNIT_TYPES[k].throughput / 1000 + 'k').join(' / ');
const NATIONAL_BILLIONS = Math.round(NATIONAL_OFFICE_VISITS.visits / 1e8) / 10;

const REGIONS = regionModel();
const MODEL = REGIONS.model;
const GEO = regionGeometry(REGIONS.regions);
const MARGIN = selectionMargin(MODEL.tested_region_counts, MODEL.selected_region_count);
const ANCHOR = fragmentationAnchor(MODEL.tested_region_counts);
const INTERVALS = weightIntervals(
  MODEL.tested_region_counts, MODEL.weights, MODEL.selected_region_count);
const FRAGILE = INTERVALS.filter((i) => i.fragile);
const WEIGHT_LABELS: Record<string, string> = {
  population_scale: 'Population scale',
  geographic_compactness: 'Geographic compactness',
  rural_workload: 'Rural workload balance',
  administrative_fragmentation: 'Administrative fragmentation'
};
/* The two regions furthest below target, which the page named neither of. */
const SMALLEST = GEO.below.slice(0, 2);
const LARGEST = GEO.above.slice(0, 2);
const M = (n: number) => (n / 1e6).toFixed(1) + 'M';
const MILLION = (n: number) => (n / 1e6).toFixed(1) + ' million';

/* Both ends of the absorption control, run over the same county file the
   client fetches. Typed prose here would be a figure nothing could check --
   the defect this whole section exists to close. */
const COUNTIES = countyDemand();
const ABSORPTION_LOW = allocateUnits(COUNTIES, NETWORK_ABSORPTION.min).total;
const ABSORPTION_HIGH = allocateUnits(COUNTIES, NETWORK_ABSORPTION.max).total;
const ABSORPTION_SPAN = ABSORPTION_HIGH - ABSORPTION_LOW;
/* the six model inputs plus the national comparator, one list, checked */
const GRADED = gradedUnitRows();
---
<BaseLayout title="National Health Assurance: Physical Care">
  <main>

  <section class="card chapter-intro">
    <span class="chapter-intro-eyebrow">Chapter 4 &middot; Physical care</span>
    <h2>Hospitals become public-service infrastructure, then care moves closer to home</h2>
    <p class="desc">The change is larger than building community clinics. It
      starts by removing hospitals from the logic of payer contracts,
      shareholder extraction, and profitable service selection. Hospitals
      receive mission-locked public-service charters and certified budgets;
      regional nonprofit administrations plan capacity and protect essential
      services. The four-unit diagnostic-treatment network then becomes the
      local front door, linked to hospitals for emergency, inpatient,
      surgical, and complex specialty care.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="card physical-problem-section">
    <span class="physical-kicker">The starting problem</span>
    <h2>Hospital administration is optimized around revenue, not the human need for care</h2>
    <p class="desc">A hospital is not an ordinary market. A patient with a
      stroke, sepsis, trauma, or a dangerous pregnancy cannot postpone the
      purchase, compare a complete price, or judge staffing quality in
      advance. When the institution has an owner entitled to residual profit,
      the easiest ways to improve returns can conflict with the public
      mission: raise prices, favor lucrative procedures, reduce labor, load
      the property with debt or rent, and close services whose social value is
      greater than their margin.</p>
    <div class="physical-problem-grid">
      <article>
        <span>Market failure</span>
        <h3>Patients cannot bargain in an emergency</h3>
        <p>Demand is involuntary, quality is hard to observe, and local
          consolidation can leave no meaningful alternative. A hospital can
          possess market power precisely when a person is least able to
          exercise consumer power.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Financialization</span>
        <h3>Private equity intensifies the extraction path</h3>
        <p>Acquisition debt, management fees, related-party transactions,
          sale-leasebacks, short holding periods, and exit targets can turn
          clinical capacity into a financial asset. The risk is borne by
          patients, workers, and the community if the facility weakens or
          closes.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Administrative load</span>
        <h3>Competing payers multiply nonclinical work</h3>
        <p>Contracting, coding, prior authorization, utilization review,
          collections, denials, and revenue-cycle teams consume labor that
          does not diagnose, treat, clean a room, or keep a service line
          ready.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="tiles physical-evidence">
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">For-profit share of Medicare-enrolled hospitals</div>
        <div class="value">36.1%</div>
        <div class="range">2023 ownership data; 49.2% nonprofit, 14.7% government</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Hospital-acquired conditions after private-equity acquisition</div>
        <div class="value">+25.4%</div>
        <div class="range">51 acquired hospitals; observational difference-in-differences study</div>
      </div>
      <div class="tile">
        <div class="label">Hospitals operating in a chain</div>
        <div class="value">56.1%</div>
        <div class="range">Consolidated control is not limited to tax status</div>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="physical-moral">
      <strong>Why profit maximization is the wrong governing rule for hospitals</strong>
      <p>Illness makes people dependent. A humane system therefore treats
        survival capacity, safe staffing, maternity care, psychiatric beds,
        trauma response, and rural access as obligations to people, not
        optional product lines. Surplus is useful when it becomes reserves,
        safer staffing, equipment, and replacement capital. It becomes
        harmful when an outside residual claimant can extract it while the
        community retains the clinical risk.</p>
    </div>
    <p class="note"><b>Important qualification:</b> ownership is not a
      patient-level quality score. Not every for-profit hospital is unsafe,
      and nonprofit status alone does not prevent monopoly pricing,
      overcompensation, or weak community benefit. The remedy is a binding
      charter, transparent accounts, related-party limits, a locked public
      mission, and enforceable quality and continuity duties for every
      participating hospital.</p>
    <p class="rollout-source physical-sources">
      Evidence:
      <a href="https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/hospital-ownership" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HHS ownership report</a>;
      <a href="https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2813379" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JAMA private-equity safety study</a>;
      <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2024/03/federal-trade-commission-department-justice-department-health-human-services-launch-cross-government" target="_blank" rel="noopener">FTC/DOJ/HHS ownership inquiry</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="physical-kicker">The administrative replacement</span>
    <h2>One public mission, three accountable layers</h2>
    <p class="desc">The new structure separates national rule-setting,
      regional capacity decisions, and hospital operations. It does not
      nationalize every clinician's employer or ask a distant national office
      to schedule local beds. It removes investor control and payer bargaining
      from the hospital operating model.</p>
    <div class="hospital-admin-flow" aria-label="Hospital administrative structure">
      <article>
        <span>National steward</span>
        <h3><abbr class="physical-acronym" title="National Hospital Stewardship Authority">NHSA</abbr></h3>
        <p>Sets charter terms, budget rules, accounting definitions,
          essential-service floors, capital standards, and national
          comparability. The charter office investigates and enforces.</p>
      </article>
      <div class="hospital-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&darr;</div>
      <article>
        <span>13 regional nonprofit administrations</span>
        <h3><abbr class="physical-acronym" title="Regional Health Administrators">RHA</abbr> operating regions</h3>
        <p>Plan beds and service lines, certify hospital budgets, maintain
          transfer and specialist capacity, coordinate emergency transport,
          and decide closures or conversions in public proceedings.</p>
      </article>
      <div class="hospital-flow-arrow" aria-hidden="true">&darr;</div>
      <article>
        <span>Chartered provider</span>
        <h3>Hospital public-service corporation</h3>
        <p>Runs clinical operations through a locally accountable nonprofit
          board. No shareholders, no balance billing, no routine facility
          fees, no sale of the mission, and no diversion of assets from care.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="hospital-charter-grid">
      <article>
        <span>Governance</span>
        <h3>A board without investor control</h3>
        <p>Patients and community members, clinical staff, labor, public
          health, tribal and local government, and, where applicable, university
          representatives. Conflicts, compensation, minutes, and votes are
          public; clinical-safety officers have protected escalation rights.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Money</span>
        <h3>A certified annual global budget</h3>
        <p>Funding covers operations, emergency readiness, protected service
          lines, safe staffing, capital replacement, teaching and research,
          high-cost cases, clinician compensation, and transition
          stabilization. It is not price times claims.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Surplus</span>
        <h3>Reinvested, reserved, or returned</h3>
        <p>Audited surplus stays in patient care, workforce, capital, or
          reserves, or returns to the regional pool. It cannot become a
          dividend, excessive management fee, affiliate rent, or leveraged
          buyout return.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Continuity</span>
        <h3>No spreadsheet-only closure</h3>
        <p>A hospital cannot close an emergency department, obstetric unit,
          psychiatric beds, trauma service, intensive-care capacity, dialysis,
          or a rural site until the region tests travel time, replacement
          capacity, workforce, equity, and transfer safety.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <div class="hospital-budget-line" aria-label="Components of a hospital global budget">
      <span>Operations</span><span>Readiness</span><span>Essential services</span>
      <span>Safe staffing</span><span>Capital</span><span>Teaching &amp; research</span>
      <span>High-cost cases</span><span>Transition reserve</span>
    </div>
    <p class="note">This budget logic has a real operating precedent:
      <abbr class="physical-acronym" title="Centers for Medicare &amp; Medicaid Services">CMS</abbr>
      describes global budgets as predetermined annual hospital revenue and
      reports that Maryland's model improved predictability, coordination, and
      Medicare savings. The national design adds stronger asset locks, service
      duties, and public governance.</p>
    <p class="rollout-source physical-sources">
      <a href="https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/key-concepts/total-cost-care-and-hospital-global-budgets" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CMS global-budget explainer</a>;
      <a href="https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/innovation-models/md-tccm" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Maryland Total Cost of Care model</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card hospital-region-section">
    <span class="physical-kicker">A reproducible regional model</span>
    <h2>Thirteen nonprofit operating regions balance scale with local accountability</h2>
    <p class="desc">A planning model aggregated all 3,144 counties to states,
      then compared {MODEL.tested_region_counts[0].regions} through
      {MODEL.tested_region_counts[MODEL.tested_region_counts.length - 1].regions}
      contiguous regional partitions. It weighted population scale
      {Math.round(MODEL.weights.population_scale * 100)}%, geographic
      compactness {Math.round(MODEL.weights.geographic_compactness * 100)}%,
      rural workload {Math.round(MODEL.weights.rural_workload * 100)}%, and the
      administrative cost of too many regions
      {Math.round(MODEL.weights.administrative_fragmentation * 100)}%. Every
      state and the District of Columbia is assigned once; state boundaries
      stay intact. The model scored {MODEL.selected_region_count} regions best.
      {LARGEST[0].region.name} and {LARGEST[1].region.name} remain above the
      {MILLION(GEO.target)} target because state integrity is a hard
      constraint, so each would use internal operating districts.</p>
    <div class="tiles hospital-model-tiles">
      {/* R212: the tile used to show the winning score with no runner-up and
          no spread, so a reader saw a clear optimum where the methodology
          published a near-tie. Both numbers were already in the file. */}
      <div class="tile"><div class="label">Selected operating regions</div><div class="value">{MARGIN.selected}</div><div class="range">composite {MARGIN.selectedTotal.toFixed(3)}, ahead of {MARGIN.runnerUp} regions at {MARGIN.runnerUpTotal.toFixed(3)} by {MARGIN.marginPct.toFixed(1)}%</div></div>
      <div class="tile"><div class="label">Population assigned</div><div class="value">{M(GEO.total)}</div><div class="range">2024 county estimates</div></div>
      <div class="tile"><div class="label">Population target per region</div><div class="value">{M(GEO.target)}</div><div class="range">achieved spread {GEO.spread.toFixed(2)}x, {GEO.smallest.id} to {GEO.largest.id}</div></div>
    </div>
    <div id="hospital-region-map"></div>
    {/* R71/R190: what the map actually drew, written by the client after it
        draws it. Static fallback text so a reader with no script is not told
        anything the page has not checked. */}
    <p class="note" id="hospital-region-integrity">Map integrity is checked when the map draws.</p>
    <div class="hospital-region-control">
      <label for="hospital-region-select">Inspect a region</label>
      <select id="hospital-region-select" aria-label="Select a nonprofit hospital administration region"></select>
      <div id="hospital-region-detail" aria-live="polite"></div>
    </div>
    <div class="hospital-score-wrap">
      <span class="physical-section-label">Candidate model score (lower is better)</span>
      <div id="hospital-region-scores" aria-label="Scores for candidate region counts"></div>
    </div>
    {/* R211: the three facts the methodology states separately and never puts
        together. The third one is arithmetic, so it is computed rather than
        asserted: if the fragmentation penalty ever stops being a parabola
        centred on the selected count, this paragraph stops claiming it is. */}
    <div class="warnbox">
      <p><b>How much of this result is the scoring.</b> Every candidate map is
        a version of the {MODEL.selected_region_count}-region one. The
        {MODEL.selected_region_count}-region map was drawn by hand from state
        contiguity, population, and recognizable operating geography; the
        lower counts merge its regions and the higher counts split them. So
        the comparison is between one hand-drawn map and
        {MODEL.tested_region_counts.length - 1} deformations of it, not
        between independent attempts at the problem.</p>
      {ANCHOR && (
        <p>And {Math.round(MODEL.weights.administrative_fragmentation * 100)}% of
          the score is defined relative to the answer. The administrative
          fragmentation term is exactly
          {ANCHOR.coefficient.toFixed(2)} times the squared distance from
          {ANCHOR.anchor} regions, so it is zero for the
          {ANCHOR.zeroAt}-region map and a penalty for every other candidate.
          A term that scores distance from an option cannot also be evidence
          for it.</p>
      )}
      <p>The {MARGIN.marginPct.toFixed(1)}% margin over {MARGIN.runnerUp}
        regions should be read that way: {MODEL.selected_region_count} regions
        is a planning judgement that the scoring supports, not the output of a
        search over possible partitions. The model does not claim otherwise;
        this page previously did, by omission.</p>
    </div>
    {/* R87: the sweep the audit asked for, run here rather than published as
        a static table, so it cannot go stale against the weights above. */}
    <div class="tbl-scroll">
      <table class="data">
        <caption class="physical-section-label">Does {MODEL.selected_region_count} regions survive a different weighting? Each row moves one weight and rescales the other three.</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Objective term</th><th>Weight</th><th>{MODEL.selected_region_count} regions wins while the weight is</th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {INTERVALS.map((i) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{WEIGHT_LABELS[i.weight]}</th>
              <td>{Math.round(i.authored * 100)}%</td>
              <td>{Number.isNaN(i.low)
                ? 'never'
                : Math.round(i.low * 100) + '% to ' + Math.round(i.high * 100) + '%'}</td>
              <td>{i.fragile ? 'flips within ' + Math.round(FRAGILE_WITHIN * 100) + ' points' : 'robust'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Read the third column against the second. Population scale
      and compactness can be weighted almost anywhere and
      {MODEL.selected_region_count} regions still scores best, so the two
      largest terms are not what decides this.
      {FRAGILE.length > 0 && (
        <Fragment> The {FRAGILE.length === 1 ? 'term that does decide it is' : 'terms that decide it are'} {FRAGILE.map((i) => WEIGHT_LABELS[i.weight].toLowerCase()).join(' and ')}, {FRAGILE.length === 1 ? 'which sits' : 'which sit'} within {Math.round(FRAGILE_WITHIN * 100)} points of a weighting that selects a different number of regions.</Fragment>
      )}
      That is the honest summary of a {MARGIN.marginPct.toFixed(1)}% margin,
      and it is a reason to treat the count as a decision to be made in public
      rather than a number the model handed over.</p>
    {/* R88: the page explained the two regions above target and said nothing
        about the two equally far below it. R213: and the balance term. */}
    <p class="note"><b>What the selected map achieved.</b> State integrity
      explains the large regions: {LARGEST.map((r) => r.region.name + ' at ' +
      r.ratio.toFixed(2) + 'x target').join(' and ')}. It does not explain the
      small ones. {SMALLEST.map((r) => r.region.name + ' is ' +
      r.ratio.toFixed(2) + 'x').join(' and ')}, and
      {SMALLEST[0].region.name} is further from target than
      {LARGEST[0].region.name} is. Those are optimizer outcomes, not
      constraint outcomes: the small regions could have been merged with a
      neighbor and were not. Smallest to largest, the spread is
      {GEO.spread.toFixed(2)}x. On rural workload, which carries
      {Math.round(MODEL.weights.rural_workload * 100)}% of the objective
      precisely to avoid concentrating sparse-area obligations, the achieved
      range runs from {(GEO.ruralLow.rural_share * 100).toFixed(1)}% rural in
      {GEO.ruralLow.name} to {(GEO.ruralHigh.rural_share * 100).toFixed(1)}% in
      {GEO.ruralHigh.name}, a {GEO.ruralSpread.toFixed(1)}x spread. Rural
      population is geographically clustered and states cannot be split, so
      some concentration is unavoidable under the hard constraints. The term
      reduces the spread; it does not remove it, and the page should not imply
      that it does.</p>
    <p class="note">These are administrative regions, not walls around
      patients. Specialty referrals, disaster response, border communities,
      and tribal or federal systems can cross them. Alaska-Washington and
      Hawaii-California are explicit administrative links, not claims of land
      contiguity. Final boundaries require tribal consultation, referral and
      drive-time analysis, workforce inventories, and public hearings.</p>
    <p class="rollout-source physical-sources">
      <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/hospital_regionalization_methodology.md"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">Model, assignments, assumptions, and sources</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="physical-kicker">Hospitals with missions larger than a region</span>
    <h2>University and specialty hospitals remain, and their special work is funded explicitly</h2>
    <div class="hospital-mission-grid">
      <article>
        <span>Academic medical centers</span>
        <h3>Keep the university mission</h3>
        <p>The university keeps academic governance, faculty appointments,
          accreditation, academic freedom, and its research enterprise. The
          clinical hospital accepts the same public-service charter as every
          other hospital. Its certified budget has separate, auditable lines
          for graduate medical education, clinical teaching, research
          infrastructure, quaternary referral care, and standby capacity, so
          those missions do not depend on maximizing procedure volume.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Children's, cancer, transplant, burn, trauma, psychiatric, and rehabilitation centers</span>
        <h3>Serve referral populations, not just local boundaries</h3>
        <p>The national steward designates capabilities that need concentrated
          expertise and minimum case volume. Regions jointly fund these
          centers through case-mix adjustments, high-cost pass-throughs, and
          capacity retainers. A specialty hospital remains when it adds needed
          capability, accepts all eligible referrals, reports outcomes, and
          does not select only profitable patients.</p>
      </article>
      <article>
        <span>Federal and sovereign systems</span>
        <h3>Coordinate without erasing legal identity</h3>
        <p><abbr class="physical-acronym" title="Veterans Health Administration">VHA</abbr>,
          military, Indian Health Service, and tribal facilities retain their
          governing authorities and treaty or statutory duties. Compacts
          establish payment, shared capacity, transfer, credentialing, and
          data rules while preserving tribal sovereignty and federal
          obligations.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
    <p class="note">Academic medicine combines patient care, medical
      education, research, and emergency preparedness. The design protects
      that bundle by paying for each mission openly rather than expecting
      profitable clinical lines to subsidize it invisibly.</p>
    <p class="rollout-source physical-sources">
      <a href="https://www.aamc.org/about-us" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Association of American Medical Colleges: academic-medicine mission</a>;
      <a href="https://www.aamc.org/about-us/institutional-membership" target="_blank" rel="noopener">teaching-hospital scale and roles</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="card">
    <span class="physical-kicker">The care continuum</span>
    <h2>Hospitals become the high-acuity layer of a four-unit diagnostic-treatment network</h2>
    <p class="desc">The regional administration sees one capacity picture.
      Community units resolve routine needs close to home, assemble complete
      workups, and obtain e-consults. Hospitals receive patients who need
      emergency, operating-room, intensive, inpatient, or complex specialty
      capability. Discharge returns the patient to a named local team instead
      of ending at the hospital door.</p>
    <div class="hospital-care-flow" aria-label="Flow between public-service hospitals and the four diagnostic-treatment unit types">
      <article class="hospital-layer">
        <span>Regional public-service hospitals</span>
        <strong><abbr class="physical-acronym" title="Emergency department">ED</abbr> &middot; surgery &middot; inpatient &middot; <abbr class="physical-acronym" title="Intensive care unit">ICU</abbr> &middot; complex specialty</strong>
        <small>Receive escalations; publish real-time capacity; discharge to a named follow-up team</small>
      </article>
      <div class="hospital-flow-spine">
        <span>Escalation &uarr;</span>
        <strong><abbr class="physical-acronym" title="Regional Health Administrators">RHA</abbr> capacity ledger + shared record + <abbr class="physical-acronym" title="Emergency medical services">EMS</abbr></strong>
        <span>&darr; discharge and specialist support</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hospital-unit-layer">
        <article><span>A</span><strong>Micro</strong><small>screen, test, vaccinate, teleclinician</small></article>
        <article><span>B</span><strong>Neighborhood</strong><small>diagnose, treat, image, follow chronic illness</small></article>
        <article><span>C</span><strong>Rural enhanced</strong><small>observe, stabilize, tele-specialty, coordinate transport</small></article>
        <article><span>D</span><strong>Urban public health</strong><small>high-volume care, behavioral health, outbreak response</small></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- The network and the count -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>The community front door, sized from need</h2>
    <p class="desc">The unit count is derived from the capacity needed to
      give urban and rural communities a practical front door to care. For
      every U.S. county, this page estimates demand as population times an
      absorption rate (Census 2024 estimates, 2020 rural classifications),
      splits it across the four unit types, and divides by per-unit
      throughput. Move the absorption slider to see how the count responds.
      For scale, the <a href={NATIONAL_OFFICE_VISITS.url} target="_blank"
      rel="noopener">{NATIONAL_OFFICE_VISITS.source}</a> counted about
      {NATIONAL_BILLIONS} billion {NATIONAL_OFFICE_VISITS.label} in
      {NATIONAL_OFFICE_VISITS.year}; emergency and hospital outpatient visits
      are on top of that, and the survey that counted those ended in 2022.</p>
    <div class="control" style="max-width:420px">
      <label>Network absorption at maturity
        <span class="val" id="units-vpc-val"></span></label>
      <input type="range" id="units-vpc"
        min={NETWORK_ABSORPTION.min} max={NETWORK_ABSORPTION.max}
        step={NETWORK_ABSORPTION.step} value={NETWORK_ABSORPTION.default}
        aria-label="Network visits per person per year">
    </div>
    <div class="tiles" id="unit-verdict"></div>
    <p class="note">Assumptions: urban visits split {pct(VISIT_SPLITS.urban.a)} micro-units,
      {pct(VISIT_SPLITS.urban.b)} neighborhood units, {pct(VISIT_SPLITS.urban.d)} urban
      public-health units (folded into Type B where a county has under
      {ALLOCATION_THRESHOLDS.urbanPopForTypeD.toLocaleString('en-US')} urban residents);
      rural visits split {pct(VISIT_SPLITS.rural.b)} neighborhood,
      {pct(VISIT_SPLITS.rural.c)} rural enhanced; throughputs of {THROUGHPUTS} visits
      per year per unit for Types A–D; counties at or above
      {pct(ALLOCATION_THRESHOLDS.ruralFloorShare)} rural, or under
      {ALLOCATION_THRESHOLDS.ruralFloorPop.toLocaleString('en-US')} people, keep at least
      one Type C, and every county gets at least one unit. Cost benchmarks come from
      urgent-care, retail-clinic, and FQHC economics in
      <a href="https://github.com/mikehalvorson/US-National-Health-Assurance-System/blob/main/research/02_hospital_clinical_workforce_education.md"
        target="_blank" rel="noopener">research file 02</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- What the count rests on -->
  <section class="card">
    <h2>What the count rests on, and how much to trust it</h2>
    <p class="desc">The numbers above are not all the same kind of thing. Some
      are measured, some are policy choices, and most were chosen because a
      capacity plan needs a number and no published one existed. Saying which
      is which is more useful than presenting them all with equal weight, so
      each input carries a confidence grade and the specific work that would
      raise it. The network cost, the unit count, and the unit-team headcount
      on the workforce chapter all descend from these rows. <strong>Not one of
      the model's own inputs is graded above low.</strong> The last row is the
      exception and is not an input: it is a published figure, stated so the
      network can be sized against something real.</p>
    <div class="tbl-scroll">
      <table class="data">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Input</th><th>Value</th><th>Confidence</th><th>What it rests on</th><th>What would settle it</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {GRADED.map((a) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{a.label}</th>
              <td>{a.value}</td>
              <td>{a.confidence}</td>
              <td>{a.url
                ? <Fragment>{a.basis} <a href={a.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">Comparator source</a>.</Fragment>
                : a.basis}</td>
              <td>{a.owner}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note">The absorption rate is the one to watch: it multiplies
      every other input. Moving it from {NETWORK_ABSORPTION.min.toFixed(2)} to
      {NETWORK_ABSORPTION.max.toFixed(2)} on the control changes the network by
      {ABSORPTION_SPAN.toLocaleString('en-US')} units, which is
      {Math.round(100 * ABSORPTION_SPAN / ABSORPTION_LOW)}% of the smaller
      network.</p>
    <p class="note">The control moves this chapter's count and this chapter's
      cost. It does <strong>not</strong> move the healthcare model's unit
      spending, which is a separate sampled input with its own range and is
      scoped to the certified network rather than to need. The two are
      deliberately independent: this page is a capacity plan, and the model
      prices a commitment. Comparing them means comparing the same number of
      units on both sides, which is what the verdict tiles above do.</p>
  </section>

